<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jason of Pherae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 370 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jason of Pherae was tyrant of Pherae in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> who built a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> army and forced Pharsalus, under Polydamas, to submit. Elected tagos, the federal leader of Thessaly, around 374 BCE, he made Thessaly briefly a great power, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> and was said to plan a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. He was assassinated in 370 BCE, and his kinsmen ruled Pherae until Philip II expelled them.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jason succeeded Lycophron I of Pherae, possibly his father, as tyrant of Pherae; Lycophron's aggressive rule had built a power base but also left instability in the city. The geographer Pausanias records that Jason knew the teachings of the Sophist Gorgias, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isocrates">Isocrates</a> claims to have been in contact with him, though none of their letters survives. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, in the Rhetoric, quotes Jason as saying that he had to do some few unjust things in order to do many just ones.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Jason enters history suddenly, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>'s account of Theban hegemony in the 370s. Xenophon quotes him boasting of six thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> from other states, picked men who could endure toils as hard as his own, unlike citizen armies with their old men and boys. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polydamas-of-pharsalus">Polydamas of Pharsalus</a> suggested that Jason did not rely on intimidation alone but offered the local aristocracies and cities a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> under a recognised tagus, with a common army to protect them from rivalry and foreign intervention.</p><p>Abroad he combined military and naval power with diplomacy. He cultivated the Molossian kingdom of Alcetas I in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and the Macedonian court of Amyntas III, entered the Second Athenian League and supported the Athenian admiral Timotheus at his trial. After intervening in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> he destroyed the fortifications of Heraclea in Trachis, so that no rival could use the city to block his movements. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Battle of Leuctra</a> he warned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> not to destroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, and later writers credit him with the half-cuirass worn by officers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Jason was killed in 370 BCE by a group of unidentified young men. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> suggests they feared he meant to take control of the Oracle of Delphi after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythian-games">Pythian Games</a>, and reports that when the people of Delphi asked the oracle what to do, it answered that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> could take care of his own shrine. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> says the killers sought fame or acted on the orders of his brother Polydorus. Alexander, possibly his son, later inherited the title of tagus and ruled harshly until the Thebans defeated him. Historians see in Jason a ruler whose early death left unrealised a regional empire like that of Dionysius I in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jason of Pherae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 370s BCE Polydamas governed the city and was forced to submit to <b>Jason of Pherae</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/pharsalus">Pharsalus</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jason of Pherae</b>, elected tagos c. 374 BCE, briefly made Thessaly a great power before his murder in 370 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/thessalian-league">Thessalian League</a></li><li><blockquote>His successor <b>Jason</b> built a large mercenary army and was elected tagos, leader of the Thessalian federation, around 374 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/tyranny-of-pherae">Tyranny of Pherae</a></li></ul></section>
